Part 4 — iCarol and Resource Data

12. iCarol Resource Harvester

The iCarol Resource Harvester is a separate technical project that supports the broader schema project.

Its purpose is to obtain resource information from an existing iCarol-based resource system and transform that information into structured data that can be examined, mapped, validated, and incorporated into the broader resource architecture.

The harvester is therefore a data acquisition and transformation project, not the definition of the Human-Centered Resource Directory Schema itself.

The schema is the broader project.

The iCarol harvester is one source and implementation pathway within that project.

---

13. Resource Data Extraction and Mapping

The harvester work examines how existing resource information can be extracted and mapped into the schema.

The process includes:

- Identifying available source fields
- Extracting resource information
- Mapping source information to schema concepts
- Preserving source information where appropriate
- Identifying missing information
- Identifying ambiguous information
- Preparing information for validation

The objective is not simply to copy an existing directory.

It is to determine what existing resource information can support the human-centered model and what information is missing.

---

14. Validation and Verification

Information obtained from an existing directory is not automatically considered accurate simply because it came from that directory.

Source provenance and information validity are separate concepts.

Information can originate from:

- An API
- A scraper
- A person conducting research
- Another verified source
- An existing resource directory

Regardless of origin, information may require review and validation before being treated as reliable resource information.

This distinction is fundamental to the accountability model.

---

15. Relationship Between the Harvester and the Schema

The iCarol Resource Harvester demonstrates how the broader schema can interact with an existing resource-information system.

The relationship is:

Existing resource data → Extraction → Mapping → Validation → Human-Centered Resource Structure

The harvester does not define the schema.

The schema provides the structure against which the extracted information can be understood.

This allows the same methodology to be applied to additional resource-data sources in the future.

---

Part 5 — Accountability as Data

16. Accountability Built Into the Data Structure

Accountability should not depend solely on a policy statement that nobody is required to enforce.

It should be embedded in the data structure itself.

If the system claims that a telephone number, eligibility requirement, service, or operating hour is current, the structure should provide a way to determine how that claim was established.

This transforms accountability from a promise into information that can be examined.

---

17. Proposed Accountability Fields

Proposed accountability fields include:

- Last verified date
- Verified by whom
- Response rate data
- User-reported accuracy scoring
- Dead-link flags
- Disconnected-number flags

These fields are currently conceptual and require further formalization.

The project will need to determine:

- Exact field definitions
- Acceptable values
- Verification methods
- Who can provide verification
- How conflicting reports are handled
- How verification history is maintained
- How accountability information is presented to users and AI systems

---

18. Measurable and Auditable Resource Information

The long-term objective is to make resource information measurable and auditable.

A person should not have to assume that a directory is accurate merely because the directory presents the information.

The data should provide evidence about its own reliability.

This creates the possibility of a resource directory in which accuracy is not simply asserted but documented.

