Part 6 — Federal Standards and Interoperability

19. Federal Standards Alignment

The project is intended to work with established interoperability standards rather than creating an isolated data ecosystem.

Human services already have standards that provide an important structural foundation.

The project therefore examines how human-centered resource information can operate within existing federal interoperability work.

---

20. HSDS as the Structural Container

HSDS — the Human Services Data Specification — is adopted as a federal ONC Interoperability Standards Advisory production standard.

It provides an established structural container for human-services resource information.

The project views HSDS as an important interoperability foundation.

The objective is not to replace the structural container.

---

21. Innovation Within the Container

The central opportunity is what happens inside the structural container.

The taxonomy, decision information, accountability information, and human-centered interpretation of resources are where additional innovation is needed.

In simplified form:

HSDS provides the container.

The Human-Centered Resource Directory Schema explores what information and relationships should exist inside that container to better represent human need and real-world access.

This approach allows innovation while maintaining interoperability.

---

22. Federal Design Standards and AI

A question was posted to the federal design standards online community regarding the inclusion of AI in federal design standards.

The purpose was to begin moving the conversation toward the role of AI in future public-information and human-service systems.

The broader question is how federal information standards can accommodate systems in which AI helps people navigate structured public information while maintaining accuracy, transparency, accountability, and appropriate information minimization.

